"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Analysis Summary</h2>\n<p>✅ Summary complete</p>\n<p>This analysis models the <strong>User Authentication Flow</strong> as a Finite State Machine (FSM) to ensure security, prevent unauthorized access, and handle edge cases like Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and account lockouts.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>1. Overview</h3>\n<p>The Authentication FSM governs the transition of a user from an anonymous state (<strong>Logged Out</strong>) to a verified state (<strong>Authenticated</strong>). Its purpose is to enforce security policies, manage session lifecycles, and handle failure modes (e.g., brute force attacks) systematically. By modeling this as an FSM, we ensure that no &quot;illegal&quot; transitions occur—such as a user reaching the Authenticated state without passing through the MFA Challenge.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>2. Key States</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th align=\"left\">State Name</th><th align=\"left\">Description</th><th align=\"left\">Type</th><th align=\"left\">Invariants</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Logged Out</strong></td><td align=\"left\">The default state where no user identity is established.</td><td align=\"left\">Initial</td><td align=\"left\"><code>SessionToken == null</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Authenticating</strong></td><td align=\"left\">A transient state where the system validates primary credentials (username/password).</td><td align=\"left\">Normal (Transient)</td><td align=\"left\"><code>ProcessingRequest == true</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>MFA Challenged</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Primary credentials are valid, but the system awaits a second factor (OTP, Biometric).</td><td align=\"left\">Normal</td><td align=\"left\"><code>PrimaryAuth == Success &amp;&amp; SecondaryAuth == Pending</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Authenticated</strong></td><td align=\"left\">The user has successfully proven their identity and holds a valid session.</td><td align=\"left\">Normal (Stable)</td><td align=\"left\"><code>SessionToken.IsValid == true</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Account Locked</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Access is denied due to security policy violations (e.g., too many failed attempts).</td><td align=\"left\">Error</td><td align=\"left\"><code>LoginEnabled == false</code></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h3>3. Critical Transitions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Credential Submission (<code>Logged Out</code> → <code>Authenticating</code>):</strong> The entry point into the security logic.</li>\n<li><strong>MFA Escalation (<code>Authenticating</code> → <code>MFA Challenged</code>):</strong> Occurs when primary credentials are correct but the policy requires a second factor.</li>\n<li><strong>Session Validation (<code>MFA Challenged</code> → <code>Authenticated</code>):</strong> The final gate where the user is granted access.</li>\n<li><strong>Security Trigger (<code>Authenticating</code> → <code>Account Locked</code>):</strong> Triggered by a counter reaching the <code>Max_Retries</code> threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Session Expiry/Revocation (<code>Authenticated</code> → <code>Logged Out</code>):</strong> The transition that cleans up sensitive data and returns the user to the initial state.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>4. Key Findings</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transient State Vulnerability:</strong> The <code>Authenticating</code> and <code>MFA Challenged</code> states are high-risk. If the FSM hangs here, it can lead to Denial of Service (DoS) for the user or resource exhaustion on the server.</li>\n<li><strong>State Leakage:</strong> Without strict exit conditions, a user might retain &quot;Partial Auth&quot; status. The FSM must ensure that failing an MFA challenge returns the user to <code>Logged Out</code>, not back to a state where they are considered &quot;half-logged in.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Lockout Persistence:</strong> The <code>Account Locked</code> state must be persistent across sessions (stored in a database) to prevent attackers from resetting the FSM by simply refreshing the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>5. Recommendations</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Implement Atomic Transitions:</strong> Ensure that the transition to <code>Authenticated</code> and the generation of the <code>SessionToken</code> happen atomically to prevent &quot;session fixation&quot; or &quot;race condition&quot; exploits.</li>\n<li><strong>Enforce Timeouts on Intermediate States:</strong> The <code>MFA Challenged</code> state should have a strict TTL (Time-to-Live). If the user doesn't provide a code within 3–5 minutes, the FSM should force a transition back to <code>Logged Out</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure Failure Paths:</strong> Ensure all &quot;Error&quot; states (like <code>Authentication Failed</code>) explicitly clear any sensitive data from memory before transitioning back to <code>Logged Out</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate Limiting at the State Level:</strong> Apply rate limiting specifically to the <code>Authenticating</code> state to mitigate brute-force attacks before they can even trigger the <code>Account Locked</code> logic.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>6. Complexity Assessment</h3>\n<p><strong>Complexity: Moderate to High.</strong>\nWhile a basic login is a simple linear flow, a robust <strong>Web Application Security</strong> FSM is complex due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-linear paths:</strong> Handling password resets, MFA bypasses, and account recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>External Dependencies:</strong> Reliance on third-party identity providers (OAuth/SAML) adds &quot;Waiting&quot; states that are outside the system's direct control.</li>\n<li><strong>Security Edge Cases:</strong> Managing concurrent sessions and global logouts requires the FSM to handle signals from other active state machines.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></div><div><div class=\"spinner-border\" role=\"status\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Loading...</span></div></div>"